Now we have established life and its many forms, and we have established it is dangerous - first contact is occurring or about to occur - what do we do? Here is a selection of preferences and protocols:

Sexy aliens?
I'm going to say this one and be very clear about it. do. not. approach. sexy. aliens. You got that? You aren't Captain Kirk, this isn't a porno, we know you've been holed up in that tin can for a long time adrift in space, and we sympathize. But the possibility of something sexy evolving on another planet light-years away from you is a near-zero possibility. The more likely scenario is the aliens have somehow gotten into your mind, or your ship's logs, or have watched a Betty Rubble fan fic porn parody from a broadcast that exited your planet a thousand years ago and just made it to their weird compound eyes. The more likely scenario is they want to suck your brains out, assimilate you, use you as a science experiment - anything that is trying to entice you is a red flag, so, my friend, pull out that plasma cannon, put your target visor down, take a few flaccid pills and get to work splattering some sexy aliens.

Hailing all frequencies?
Do not respond to hails. It could be a virus, or they could be probing you for weaknesses - or even worse, TRYING TO BEFRIEND YOU. Even if you are lucky enough to be the person in the .0003 percent chance of first contact scenarios and the aliens want to be your buddies and have an awkward greeting, you must consider protocol 5323.322 of the galactic provers manual and know that life often comes in clusters - so the aliens are friendly - what about their computer? Do they have parasites? Are they the dangling string of a deep space void-monster anglerfish? HAVE YOU CONSIDERED THEIR BACTERIA? Most first contact scenarios, even if they go well, a handshake can carry billions of unknown pathogens your onboard immune system has never seen. Within hours you are more than likely going to dissolve into a petri dish of Jell-O as the bacteria from two worlds figure things out - your bacteria are smarter and better, you say? That's nice -- only 26% of your brain has dissolved before bacterial homeostasis has occurred, and you can never go home again as you have just changed into a plague-bearing proto-sci-fi zombie.
So, my friends, if you see another ship, hide. If they notice you, look big and quietly back away. If they follow, check out their weapon systems with your peepers. Throw some radio waves in their direction and check the spectral analysis of their hull (don't use the word scans - what the fuck does that even mean? Do the writers know how to science?) Look at what is emitting from their rocket engines, ...and then unload every weapon you have. And be aware of the splat, as this can carry pathogens.

My best friend just met some alien and wants to tell me about it.

Kill them, preferably from a safe distance - then drop their body in a star. Find out where your friend met the aliens, then quarantine that region of space. What are the odds your friend met nice aliens, really? Really? It is more likely they have been taken over, knowingly or unknowingly carrying your doom. It's okay if they beg and bring up the good times you've had together; say, you know, then remember the good times. Kill them anyway.

Hey! I just met some friendly aliens. What is the protocol for this?
WHAAAAAT ARE YOU DOING? Did you skip through these chapters? Quarantine yourself immediately. Look for strange fungal growths. DO NOT TELL ANYONE ABOUT THIS. Technically it is a violation of galactic rule 453434.2323 to hide disclosure of alien life, but this book doesn't apply to these rules - it is here for your survival, and now your fellow (insert species here) will dissect and kill you to make sure you are safe - there will be no fame, no one will congratulate your successful first contact. Treat it like an STD. And if your friends know, it will be bad. There is no confiding in anyone. Pretend you just murdered your very best friend. You don't want to think about what will happen if you speak those words aloud. If someone asks, deny. If someone is about to kill you, beg. Bring up the very deepest childhood memories, appeal to their humanity. They can't kill their best friend, can they?

Take me to your leader
On some occasions first contact finds you - what do you do when a space armada shows up at your doorstep? Firstly - don't panic - okay, a little bit of panic is okay, maybe even healthy. But after the panic, we need to take into account the lessons we have learned in this manual. The possibility of technological equivalence is so small it is basically null, and you are likely going to die or be subverted in some way. Pray to your gods, say goodbye to your loved ones...then fight dirty. Really dirty.
Welcome them, and then your first order should be to find a greasy, unwashed sacrifice of a human - perhaps a Native American, give the native human some smallpox blankets and then cough in their mouth for good measure, send them to the main ship hub as biological warfare - do not tell this poor sacrifice of a human that you are doing this, in the event the aliens can read minds. After this is done, do not allow this individual to return (biological warfare is a two-way street) - and also, who knows what uncomfortable probing or rearranging this individual has just undergone. Thank them for their service and vaporize them if they attempt to return - you will be doing this person a service. Then, wait, be quiet, ignore continued attempts at communication - say it's the Lord's day of rest; perhaps they will understand. If you are lucky, they will get sick and leave. If you are unlucky, their ships in orbit will start to decay with no one at the helm; send rockets up to gently push their ships into the heart of the sun. If you are really unlucky, then they survive and overcome. This is where you really need to worry. You might try sending every rocket in your arsenal at their ships as a mild deterrent, but in all likelihood, if the aliens have the technology to travel between star systems you are likely effed, and having no other recourse, you must abandon planet. Hopefully they are more interested in your biosphere or some unique resource found in the crust of your planet (let's face it - yeah right, there are many lifeless rocks and asteroids saturated with better resources than whatever garbage you are hoarding in your mediocre gravity well). Perhaps the nukes you sent into space at the very least will mask your exit strategy or keep them busy. Whatever it is, if the aliens call and you can't fight them off, it's macht nichts, little world, time to find a new one, and maybe don't go sending radio waves out into the dark next time, mkay?

I just got contacted by aliens. They claim that their name is SETI or some s***.

Full stop. We have gone over this. We understand the protocols if an alien is directly hailing you. But what if the alien is broadcasting our location? These ignorant motherfuckers are likely to be a type one civilization that has not yet left its biosphere. Emergent civilizations are very dangerous, but in a situation like this, we have things that we can do.
First order of business: open your warp-capable transdimensional spaceship and head to their planet. Park yourself at a safe distance. Preferably just outside their atmosphere, outside the ability of their ships to reach you. Perhaps just outside the orbit of their small moon if they are sufficiently advanced or you like a bit of cover. Scan them, evaluate them, ignore their hails. And then when you have enough information on the locations of their cities and population centers...bomb the shit out of them. Don't know how? You can get a satellite to orbit their planet in an asymmetrical orbit, covering a new strip of land every day, eventually and systematically covering every inch of their planet - here's the math to do this. Antimatter bombs, or just plain old nukes, should do nicely. Mass drivers also function, although you must be careful not to accelerate any mass too fast; if material exits their biosphere into space, it can make contact with your ship. Whatever weapon you choose, make sure to choose something very sterilizing. As noted earlier, it is nearly impossible to kill the biosphere itself. Lower order life will survive. If the aliens are tenacious enough, or their bunkers deep enough, they can find a way. The goal is not to eradicate the macroscale life; it is to destroy their ambitions and dreams of space, removing the possibility of them going out into the universe and spreading their diseases. Competing for resources, or just in general being a liability. By the time they recover it will be thousands of years, you will have reached a technological singularity and safely sublimed into the background of space by the time they grow up technologically and come back swinging.

The air gap method
This is a strategy that was adapted from old-school voting machines. You will notice one of the most prevalent things repeated throughout this manual is the issue with contact being a vector. So over time, some methods have been developed that have had success in some situations, but it is important to understand when to use it, the benefits, and the limitations. If you are worried about hostility or contamination - use a probe, a subordinate, a friend, radar, sonar, your mother, whatever. You send them out to make contact, and they relay their findings. This has the benefit of having an air gap or quarantine against lower order contaminants like bacteria or feral nanobots, but

The benefits are insulation, which protects you, but you need to be aware that the insulator person/probe is vulnerable to compromise - they can send back false data, reveal your position, relay information that can reveal your location light-years away hiding behind that brown dwarf, become a compromised node that spreads information viruses, and generally just tell the aliens all about you. Excellent idea, some success; be wary. In laboratory experiments this method has a 3 percent success rate - in other words, it is really only successful if the aliens are a lower order than you or not hostile.

The next obvious escalation in this series, my dear reader, is something you should expect by now, using your monkey brain for pattern recognition, having seen the way the words here move forward. The air gap theory evolves over time, and becomes a vacuum or quantum foam gap. Sometimes the vacuum is filled with some of the most hostile contingencies imaginable to prevent transmission of hostile biologics or data. These things can be overcome with sufficient technology - and sufficiently advanced technology that wants you dead, really, just lay down and die - but if you give in to your survival instincts and use that squishy fibrous or computer-enhanced mass of yours, the next step up from a probe is a patsy. A patsy is a creature or machine or entity or object that UNWITTINGLY initiates first contact - we have no statistically significant data on this subject, as a successful patsy situation goes completely unnoticed. Methods can include, but are not limited to, affecting the gravitational trajectory subtly of an asteroid or heavenly body - lobbing a benign object at the subject of your curiosity can tell you a lot about them - if they dodge, they have survival instincts; if the asteroid liquefies, evaporates, or changes trajectory heading right back to you...well...you are effed, bro. Another option of passive first contact is to identify a lower order lifeform, infiltrate their information systems enough to subtly nudge the general masses' opinions.


When first contact isn't actually first contact.
Sometimes referred to as "columbusing," we actually don't know where this name came from, but it is theorized to come from some pre-industrial ruffle clowns. Anyhow, it is a common theme for travelers to misunderstand and think they are the first to contact a civilization. It is frequent in spacefaring civs - especially distributed non-assimilated species - this poses some interesting scenarios that aren't exactly central to first contact but must be acknowledged for the sake of completeness. 1 - If first contact has occurred - but you see no signs of it - it may indicate it did not go well for the initial contactees. 2 - The definition of first contact can be broad or loose depending - first contact for you, your species, or society? First contact with an alien in general? All these different scenarios have different challenges and guidelines - refer to galactic manual subsection S438049238.3 for deeper reading. For the sake of this book we are going to keep this short - look for terms like "hello again" - this may be an indication the species you are interacting with doesn't know how to differentiate between faces, or they may assume your society has no individualism, or is a hive mind or singular distributed entity.
If you encounter a new alien race or intelligence, it may be good to pull out your handy hand terminal and do a targeted search to verify you are actually meeting the species for the first time - if contact with the race has occurred previously there may be valuable insights about their taxonomy, whether they are going to eat you, or whether you can eat them.
